## Models carried

| Model | Where it comes from | Introduced or first used here | Used again here | After the band |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| feelings faces | SEL K to 2 (kindergarten unit 2), with the 1 to 5 strength scale SEL 3 to 5 adds in grade 3; grade 6 adds the exact word among close ones (g6-sa-name-feelings), so the card becomes the feelings scale: a word plus how strong it is | Unit 2 (grown) | Units 3, 4, 10, 11, 13, 15, 16 | Grade 9 reads a pattern in the feelings and plans for it |
| calm-down steps | SEL K to 2 and SEL 3 to 5 (four steps, stop, breathe, name it, choose, with a menu of three body tools; the shared calm-down card, STANDARD section 7); grade 6 grows them to five, stop, breathe, name it, pick a tool, go on, with a menu of four tools matched to settings (g6-sm-calm-down) | Unit 3 (grown) | Units 10, 15 | Grade 9 builds and judges its own plan from the steps |
| stop-think-choose | SEL K to 2 and SEL 3 to 5 (four steps, stop, list two or three choices, think what each would cause, choose and check); units 1 and 2 use that form; grade 6 grows it to five steps, stop, list options, weigh each, choose, write the reason (g6-rds-careful-thinking) | Unit 1 (grade 5 form); unit 3 (grown) | Units 2, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14, 18 | Grade 9 judges a whole decision process against a shortcut |
| talk turn card | SEL K to 2 and SEL 3 to 5 (four steps, listen, say back, ask a question, add your idea); grade 6 adds a first step, choose how to talk (in person, a call or a message) (g6-rel-talk-listen) | Unit 4 (grown) | Units 6, 7, 11, 17, 18 | Grade 9 plans and checks a whole talk with it |
| I-statement frame | SEL K to 2 and SEL 3 to 5, the shared I-statement (STANDARD section 7) in four parts, "I feel ___ when ___ because ___. I need ___." | Unit 4 | Units 6, 7, 8, 11, 14, 17, 18 | Carried into grades 9 to 12 talks, limits and repairs |
| problem size meter | SEL K to 2 and SEL 3 to 5 (tiny, small, medium, big, each with who to go to); grade 6 sizes new-school problems on it and picks the helper that fits | Unit 1 | Units 6, 11, 12, 17, 18 | Grade 9 builds a map of where help is |
| goal steps | SEL K to 2 and SEL 3 to 5 (four steps, goal, steps, block and plan, check; the shared goal card, STANDARD section 7, whose rungs health's goal-plan and PE's goal cycle also use) | Unit 1 | Units 6, 8, 10, 12, 14, 15, 18 | Grade 9 designs a shared goal with its review |

New in this band and kept all band: the refusal options card (unit 4: a plain no, a no with a reason, a way out of the place; the refusal steps of STANDARD section 7), the fairness questions card (from SEL 3 to 5, turned on online and offline norms in unit 5), the who-is-affected map (unit 5, its reach widened to an online audience) and the upstander options card (unit 6: speak up, support the person afterward, get an adult, report online). Within each year a card is grown only in the unit named here and used unchanged after it.

## Units

| # | Year | Unit and compelling question | Days | Skills | Owned standards | Core link and routines | What students learn | Why this unit, here | Check set adds | Sensitive topics and teacher notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 6 | Finding my way. *How do I get organized in a new school, read what each place asks, and choose who to ask when I am stuck?* | 10 | g6-rel-ask-offer-help; g6-sa-i-can-try; g6-sm-plan-and-tidy; g6-sm-stay-on-task; g6-soc-read-situation |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e6-collab-discussion, the discussion norms the class sets in ELA's first unit, used in every pair and group talk here | Two groups. Group 1, getting organized: a weekly planner set up for an invented first week of middle school (six classes, a locker, two clubs), two ways to keep materials compared, one picked and the reason written (SM5); three ways to stay on task when an invented group chat keeps buzzing (phone in another room, a timer, a study partner), weighed against what friends expect, one picked (SM3); two first steps on the goal steps card for a character's hard first task (a band tryout, a first lab report), one chosen with a place to ask for help (SA7). Group 2, reading the new school: three new-school settings (a crowded hallway, a club meeting, a lunch table) read for what each asks and the chance it offers, and a way to act chosen on stop-think-choose in its grade 5 form (SO7); an invented sixth grader's problems (a lost schedule, a stuck locker, a worry that does not go away) sized on the problem size meter, the helper that fits chosen among a friend, a teacher, the counselor and a message to a family adult, and the ask rehearsed with the teacher playing the helper (RS8). Start-of-band readiness: watched in session 1's first task, one card worked with stop-think-choose, the meter and the goal steps in their grade 5 forms, marked on the class sheet; no separate test. End-of-unit evidence: a written scenario page on unseen cases (the planner choice and its reason; the setting's clue; the helper that fits a sized problem) and the ask rehearsed in pairs from an unseen card. Carry it out: "plan it, then start it" when homework is set, the weekly planner checked each Monday. Family activity: each person shows one way they keep track of a busy week. Tools: the planner page, the goal steps card, stop-think-choose (grade 5 form), the problem size meter, scene cards. Curio Class writes the planner, the cards, the scenario page and the family note. Provisional cost for 30: about $9 printing. Out: the five-step stop-think-choose (unit 3). Begun here, owned in grade 8 (unit): CASEL.Self-Management.5 (15), CASEL.Self-Management.3 (15), CASEL.Social Awareness.7 (14), CASEL.Relationship Skills.8 (17), CASEL.Self-Awareness.7 (14). | First because the move to middle school is the year's opening task (STANDARD section 3, "New school, new me"): planning, focus and knowing who to ask are needed in the first weeks, and every later rehearsal leans on them. Health 6 to 8 unit 2 (reaching reliable help, weeks 4 to 6) comes alongside; SEL's helper choice comes first and health adds what gets in the way (Cross-subject, Health). Two groups and 10 sessions: five sub-skills, organizing and help. Sessions 1 to 10 of the year, weeks 1 to 5. | Candidate: the readiness task kept to one card inside session 1; the planner page tested for six real-length class names on one sheet | No student says which problems are their own; a worry that does not go away is sized "big", with the counselor as the helper, and any student who names one is followed up privately. No student's phone, chat or home routine is asked about. Disclosure: health's steps in every unit (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something": the school's reporting route and the counselor). Agent reads: sensitivity. |
