## Course-level checks

Each year holds a mid-year and a year-end check, and each check is the end-of-unit Check of the unit before it (units 3, 6, 9, 12, 15, 18), with its own Respond session after it. Each check takes a written scenario page on unseen invented cases (every sampled item a character's choice, never the student's own) and one watched rehearsal per pair from an unseen card, marked on the check's own sheet, which is kept only until its respond session. School days are counted from the start of each year (course day n falls on school day 2.5n, rounded up); grades 6 to 8 are judged by school day, and week 32 is a finals week. None falls on a core course-level check day for its grade (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md).

| Check | After unit | Check days | Respond days | What it checks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Grade 6 mid-year check (session 30, school day 75, week 15; respond session 31, school day 78, week 16) | 3 | 1 | 1 | Unit 3's end-of-unit assessment: the five calm-down steps and a chosen response, the five-step stop-think-choose on a crowded-night case, a practice-log fix. Samples units 1 and 2 on new cases: a planner choice and its reason, the helper that fits a sized problem, the exact feeling word with its strength and clue, the best-supported thought. Forms: one written scenario page and one watched rehearsal per pair. |
| Grade 6 year-end check (session 63, school day 158, week 32; respond session 64, school day 160, week 32) | 6 | 1 | 1 | Unit 6's end-of-unit assessment: the safest upstander option for a new case, a team split defended, solutions weighed against criteria, a goal's first step. Samples units 4 and 5 on new cases: the channel and the refusal that fit, a question before taking a side, questions before sharing, who a post reaches. Forms: one written scenario page and one watched rehearsal per pair. |
| Grade 7 mid-year check (session 30, school day 75, week 15; respond session 31, school day 78, week 16) | 9 | 1 | 1 | Unit 9's end-of-unit assessment: a snap judgment checked against evidence, the fairness questions on a new norm, the other side's best case, a claim checked against a sample. Samples units 7 and 8 on new cases: one group seen from two sides, credit worded to land, a refusal with an alternative, a moment read from a second side. Forms: one written scenario page and one watched rehearsal per pair. |
