# Course frame: Social and Emotional Learning, Grades 6 to 8

Course `social-and-emotional-learning-grades-6-to-8` (catalog id `sel-6-8`), grade 6, grade 7 and grade 8, one band course taught over three school years. Draft frame, written 2026-11-25 on the shape of the pilot (docs/courses/social-and-emotional-learning-grades-k-to-2/frame.md) and its two reads; the scope and sequence sets the ordered days and school-day positions later.

## The course in brief

- **Units.** 18, six a year (STANDARD section 3): units 1 to 6 in grade 6 ("New school, new me"), 7 to 12 in grade 7 ("Pressure and belonging"), 13 to 18 in grade 8 ("Choices that count"), numbered through the band so a seam or a check can name one unit without a year beside it. The Year column of the Units table names the year. Each year teaches all 40 sub-skills of the five competencies (self-awareness, self-management, social awareness, relationship skills, responsible decision-making): grade 6 chooses among options, grade 7 takes in another person's view, grade 8, the owner year, chooses when every option costs something. Each year's order and unit titles follow STANDARD section 3 and the progression rows' unit notes (data/progressions/sel.csv). A year is whole on its own: it holds its own mid-year and year-end checks, and no year waits on another course.
- **Days.** 192 sessions: 64 a year, two sessions a week over 32 weeks, 30 minutes a session in grades 6 to 8 (STANDARD section 2), so 60 minutes a week. Unit sizes follow Desk q-fix-sel-1 exactly (For the lead 1): each year has four three-group units of 11 sessions (3 groups of Understanding, Fluency, Application, then the unit's Check and Respond) and two two-group units of 10 (2 groups of Understanding, two Fluency sessions, Application, then Check and Respond), 4 x 11 + 2 x 10 = 64. The course-level checks are the Check and Respond sessions of each year's third and sixth unit (frame-write, after-core courses: a course-level check right after a unit is that unit's end-of-unit assessment when it covers the unit), so those units show 9 (three-group) or 8 (two-group) days in the Units table and the check and its respond carry the other 2: each year is 60 unit days and 4 check days. No unit runs over 25 days, so none has a mid-unit assessment. Sizes by year: grade 6 runs 10, 11, 8 + 2, 11, 11, 9 + 2; grade 7 runs 11, 10, 8 + 2, 11, 11, 9 + 2; grade 8 runs 10, 11, 9 + 2, 11, 10, 9 + 2 (grade 8 puts its second two-group unit fifth so its mid-year check lands on school day 78, clear of the grade 8 science check on day 75). Short path, one session a week (32 a year, STANDARD C2, Desk L43: fewer minutes, never fewer units): a three-group unit is 5 (3 sessions where each group's Understanding and Fluency share one, 1 Application, 1 Check and Respond together); a two-group unit is 6 (2 groups of 2 shared sessions, 1 Application, 1 Check and Respond), 4 x 5 + 2 x 6 = 32. In units 3 and 6 of a year the shared Check and Respond session is the course-level check, check and respond in one session. Readiness: grade 6 unit 1 opens with a start-of-band readiness check watched inside session 1's first task (the grade 5 forms of stop-think-choose, the problem size meter and the goal steps card used on one card), marked on the class sheet, no separate test; a class that did not take SEL 3 to 5 gets each card's grade 5 form taught in the first ten minutes of the group that first uses it (the leave-out bridge, below). Every later unit's rows assume the year before's rows, which the course taught, so each unit opens with retrieval inside the session opening, not a readiness check. A "Given:" readiness item is used only where a school left out the earlier unit (STANDARD section 5.6).
- **Inside the 30 minutes.** Every session has one opening of about 5 minutes (one retrieval item or returning skill with its card, never two openings), about 20 minutes of teaching and rehearsal, and about 5 minutes to mark the class sheet, write the one-line reason the session asks for and collect cards. An Understanding session names and models at most three new sub-skills, each with its card step and one invented case every student works once; every group in this course holds two or three sub-skills, so no unit needs a second Understanding session. Units with a class project (6, 12, 18) take its planning and carrying out from the 20 minutes of their Application sessions, and their unit plans say how.
- **Standard.** CASEL 5 competencies, as written in data/standards/casel-5.txt (40 sub-skills, the five competency names and the codes CASEL.Competency.N). Grade 8 owns every code for the band; grade 6 and grade 7 rows begin each one (~ in the rows), so their Owned standards cells are empty and What students learn names each begun code with its grade 8 owner unit. Each grade 8 owner row teaches the whole of its sub-skill, the one step its code names, at the grade 8 demand (a cost on every option). The seventh column, Core link and routines, holds for SEL the unit's one link to a core course of the same year (bar part 9 item 9): a lean the teacher may use, not a strand. Library: AASL.V.D.1 is taught with SM3 (owner unit 15), AASL.V.D.2 with SA8 (unit 15) and AASL.III.D.2 with RS4 (unit 18) in every SEL band (library STANDARD section 4, Desk q-fix-library-1; data/plans/library-excluded.csv names them placed in SEL). The three owner rows name the codes in their notes; Pending(scripts/progression.py, scripts/frame.py#AASL ids accepted for SEL): the codes move into the rows' standard cells and these units' Owned standards (For the lead 3). Check set: each unit plan checks itself against APPROACH's kinds of day, the lens (docs/agents/lenses/sel.md) and the bar's part 9; the "Check set adds" column names what the writer expects that to add.
- **Base text.** None. Every case, scene card, message thread, data page, step card and written page is Curio Class's own, with invented students, families, apps and towns; no real app, platform or brand is named. The one settled case from history (school segregation, struck down in 1954, units 9 and 16) is taught from a public primary-source page the sensitivity agent reads before the unit plan (the National Archives page on Brown v. Board of Education, https://www.archives.gov/milestone-documents/brown-v-board-of-education, named by this writer, not read; For the lead 11). The clashes of work habits in units 6, 7 and 16 are between invented students' own habits (how blunt feedback is, when to talk, how to split work), never a culture's way, so no cultural source is needed.
- **Shared steps with health and PE.** The talk turn card, calm-down steps, I-statement frame, goal steps card and refusal options use the one set of words in STANDARD section 7 ("Shared steps"), so a student meets one card across SEL, health and PE. Where health or PE teaches a card first in a year, the SEL unit opens with it as retrieval where the school runs that course, and teaches it itself where it does not (Cross-subject, Health and PE).
- **Leave-out.** A school may leave out a unit (STANDARD section 5.6). Where a later unit uses a card the left-out unit made or grew, that unit's first Understanding session teaches the card's one-step form itself in its first ten minutes, in place of the retrieval opening and part of that session's rehearsal, and claims none of the left-out unit's sub-skills: the feelings scale with the exact word (unit 2; first needed by units 4, then 10, 15); the five-step calm-down steps and the five-step stop-think-choose (unit 3; units 4, 5, 6, then 8, 10, 15); the talk turn card's first step, choose how to talk, the refusal options and the I-statement (unit 4; units 6, 8, 11, 14, 17); the fairness questions and the who-is-affected map (unit 5; units 9, 12, 13, 16); the upstander options (unit 6; units 11, 18). Grade 6 units 1 and 2 use the grade 5 forms of the cards, which SEL 3 to 5 taught; the readiness check and the same ten-minute bridge cover a class that did not take it.
- **Kinds of day.** APPROACH "Kinds of day": Understanding (the skill shown on an invented case, then the steps named on a step card), Fluency (the steps rehearsed in pairs or small groups on new cards until quick), Application (the steps used on a new case, a message thread or a real class task, with the choice and its reason written), Check (a written scenario page from unseen cases and a watched rehearsal from an unseen card, against look-fors), Respond (a short reteach for the step not yet seen, and the rehearsal again). Every session rehearses; no session is posters and discussion alone. Each year is taught a new way, not the same lessons again: grade 6 lays options side by side and picks one, grade 7 runs each case from a second person's side, grade 8 puts a price on every option and judges the choice afterward.
- **Evidence.** A class sheet per unit, the step marked "seen" or "not yet" on the day it was seen. It records a step, not a rating of the student; no behavior rating of any student is collected (STANDARD C). It is the teacher's working paper and is thrown away at the end of its unit (APPROACH, Daily evidence); a course-level check keeps its own sheet only until its respond session, then it is thrown away too. Feelings in a check are a character's, never the student's own. Private pages (SA7, SA9) stay with the student, checked only that the steps were followed; SA3, SA4, SA7 and SA9 are checked on invented cases or private pages, and the course never records a student's own answer to SA7 or SA9 (STANDARD section 3). End-of-unit evidence in every unit: the written scenario page is taken by the whole class in the Check session; the watched rehearsal from an unseen card is marked across the unit's last Application session and the Check session, pairs not yet seen going first in the Check (no score from a session where a skill is first taught). In units 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 and 18 the course-level check is that Check session. On the short path the page sits in the shared Check and Respond session and the rehearsal is marked in the Application session. Any student may do a rehearsal with the teacher alone or with a card, not in front of the class (APPROACH, supports).
- **Evidence base.** No study has tried this course; it is never called evidence-based or rated. The course page carries the logic model (C16) and this line (bar part 9 item 8; APPROACH, "Where the evidence is thin").
- **Carry it out and families.** Each unit names one cue the teacher uses outside the session ("Carry it out" in What students learn) and one family activity, sent in the unit's family note. Family notes are the only translated pages, in English and Spanish (STANDARD section 2); student pages are English only for now. A school may leave out a unit; its family note and the course page then name the left-out sub-skill ids the course does not claim that year (STANDARD section 5.6).
- **Fidelity and before you teach.** A paper fidelity checklist per session (did it run as planned, which part was cut) and a "before you teach" note opening each unit's teacher page: the skill as an adult uses it and one thing to model. Not training, and not claimed as such (bar part 9 items 11 and 13).
- **Equity.** Families of every kind appear in model pages, LGBTQ families included; no student is asked about a family, culture, faith or home language by name, or asked to teach or explain their own culture. SA6 (snap judgments) is checking one's own guess on invented cases, the wrong guess resting on a neutral trait (new to the team, a clothing style, a quiet manner), never blame of a group. SO6 uses settled history and invented school norms only. Student voice in school life (a class proposal in units 6 and 12, a school improvement carried out in unit 18), with no cause picked by the course and the analysis of causes left to social studies (bar part 9 item 12).
- **Devices and paper path.** No device for students. Every session runs on the paper path: step cards, scene cards, printed message threads and invented feeds, data pages, planners and class charts, printed or drawn by hand. Online life is taught on paper copies of invented screens; no student opens an account, an app or a real feed. Any online tool a later unit plan adds has a paper path beside it and needs no student account.
- **Tools and costs.** Kept all band: the talk turn card, feelings scale (the grade 5 feelings faces with their 1 to 5 strength), calm-down step card and tool menu, stop-think-choose card, goal steps card, problem size meter and I-statement frame (all from SEL 3 to 5, grown here in units 2, 3 and 4), plus the refusal options card, fairness questions card, who-is-affected map and upstander options card. Each unit names its tools, what Curio Class writes and a provisional cost for 30 students; printing runs about $8 to $10 a unit, about $55 to $60 a year, plus about $5 to $10 of project supplies in units 6, 12 and 18 and $4 once for a two-minute sand timer per group (unit 13). No supply ceiling is set for SEL (For the lead 10); these are planning figures, not quotes.
- **Safety.** No movement game or tool hazard in this course. The project units (6, 12, 18) stay inside the school day and grounds, with an adult present; any sign-up sheet, poster or survey goes up only where the school allows. Online cases are paper only, so no student is put in contact with anyone. Each project unit names its controls; the safety agent reads units 6, 12 and 18 against the school's own grounds and activity policy.
- **Disclosure.** Asking for help, stress, pressure, friendship, conflict and upstander units can bring a disclosure, and middle school cases (a worry that does not go away, a friend's secret, a mocked classmate) make one more likely. Every unit follows health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Health owns substances, body safety, sleep and mental health topics, self-harm and suicide; SEL never teaches them, and its cases never use them.
- **Sensitivity reads.** Every unit gets the fresh sensitivity agent's read (docs/pipeline/briefs/sensitivity-read.md); no outside reader is planned (Cory, 2026-11-14). Before units 9 and 16 the agent reads the Brown v. Board source named under Base text and checks the case is taught as settled history; in units 6, 7 and 16 it checks each clash of habits is two invented students' own, never tied to a culture or group; in units 2, 7 and 14 it checks identity, home language and values work stays on invented characters, the student's own page optional and private; in units 5, 8 and 13 it checks no real platform, trend or person is named. Units 6, 12 and 18 also get the safety agent's read. The Spanish family notes get a translation agent's read (For the lead 9). The course-level checks get the answer-key agent's read.

## Seams

### Picked up from Social and Emotional Learning, Grades 3 to 5

Every grade 6 row assumes its grade 5 owner row, which SEL 3 to 5 teaches (its frame, docs/courses/social-and-emotional-learning-grades-3-to-5/frame.md, "Handed on to Social and Emotional Learning, Grades 6 to 8", gives the grade 5 units below, numbered through that band). The grade 6 unit opens with the grade 5 form as retrieval; the readiness check in unit 1 and the leave-out bridge cover a class that did not take SEL 3 to 5.

| Row here (unit) | Assumes (SEL 3 to 5 unit) | What grade 6 adds |
|---|---|---|
| g6-sm-plan-and-tidy (SM5, unit 1) | g5-sm-plan-and-tidy (14) | From one backward plan for one project to a system chosen among options across many teachers' deadlines. |
| g6-sm-stay-on-task (SM3, unit 1) | g5-sm-stay-on-task (14) | The focus plan becomes a choice among options against an online pull. |
| g6-soc-read-situation (SO7, unit 1) | g5-soc-read-situation (13) | From naming what one new place will ask to choosing an action in places that ask different things. |
| g6-rel-ask-offer-help (RS8, unit 1) | g5-rel-ask-offer-help (16) | From asking one helper despite embarrassment to choosing the helper that fits the size of the problem. |
| g6-sa-i-can-try (SA7, unit 1) | g5-sa-i-can-try (14) | A backup plan becomes a choice between first steps with a reason. |
| g6-sa-who-i-am (SA1, unit 2) | g5-sa-who-i-am (18) | From how identity stays and changes across a move to choosing what to show in different groups. |
| g6-sa-my-strengths (SA2, unit 2) | g5-sa-my-strengths (18) | From using strengths in one new setting to choosing which strength fits which task. |
| g6-sa-tell-truth (SA4, unit 2) | g5-sa-tell-truth (18) | Honesty chosen among tempting options, with the reason on paper. |
| g6-sa-what-interests-me (SA9, unit 2) | g5-sa-what-interests-me (14) | From judging one interest's worth to choosing among new ones. |
| g6-sa-name-feelings (SA3, unit 2) | g5-sa-name-feelings (13) | From naming mixed feelings to choosing the exact word and its strength. |
| g6-sa-feel-think-link (SA5, unit 2) | g5-sa-feel-think-link (13) | One balanced thought becomes a choice among thoughts tested against the facts. |
| g6-sm-big-feelings (SM1, unit 3) | g5-sm-big-feelings (13) | The steps used to choose among responses, with onlookers as the pressure. |
| g6-sm-calm-down (SM2, unit 3) | g5-sm-calm-down (13) | One tool kept for one change becomes a menu matched to settings. |
| g6-sa-skills-grow (SA8, unit 3) | g5-sa-skills-grow (14) | From explaining a setback to choosing the fix among options. |
| g6-rds-careful-thinking (RD5, unit 3) | g5-rds-careful-thinking (15) | Careful thinking done on options laid side by side. |
| g6-rds-good-for-us (RD6, unit 3) | g5-rds-good-for-us (18) | Well-being weighed across several options, with online time as the pull. |
| g6-rel-talk-listen (RS1, unit 4) | g5-rel-talk-listen (16) | The card used to choose the channel, not only to take turns. |
| g6-rel-friendships (RS2, unit 4) | g5-rel-friendships (16) | From keeping one friendship across a change to choosing among friendship paths. |
| g6-rel-settle-disagreements (RS5, unit 4) | g5-rel-settle-disagreements (16) | The steps used with a choice of how and when to settle. |
