| 1 | 6 | Finding my way. *How do I get organized in a new school, read what each place asks, and choose who to ask when I am stuck?* | 10 | g6-rel-ask-offer-help; g6-sa-i-can-try; g6-sm-plan-and-tidy; g6-sm-stay-on-task; g6-soc-read-situation |  | None (SEL). Core link (bar item 9): ela:e6-collab-discussion, the discussion norms the class sets in ELA's first unit, used in every pair and group talk here | Two groups. Group 1, getting organized: a weekly planner set up for an invented first week of middle school (six classes, a locker, two clubs), two ways to keep materials compared, one picked and the reason written (SM5); three ways to stay on task when an invented group chat keeps buzzing (phone in another room, a timer, a study partner), weighed against what friends expect, one picked (SM3); two first steps on the goal steps card for a character's hard first task (a band tryout, a first lab report), one chosen with a place to ask for help (SA7). Group 2, reading the new school: three new-school settings (a crowded hallway, a club meeting, a lunch table) read for what each asks and the chance it offers, and a way to act chosen on stop-think-choose in its grade 5 form (SO7); an invented sixth grader's problems (a lost schedule, a stuck locker, a worry that does not go away) sized on the problem size meter, the helper that fits chosen among a friend, a teacher, the counselor and a message to a family adult, and the ask rehearsed with the teacher playing the helper (RS8). Start-of-band readiness: watched in session 1's first task, one card worked with stop-think-choose, the meter and the goal steps in their grade 5 forms, marked on the class sheet; no separate test. End-of-unit evidence: a written scenario page on unseen cases (the planner choice and its reason; the setting's clue; the helper that fits a sized problem) and the ask rehearsed in pairs from an unseen card. Carry it out: "plan it, then start it" when homework is set, the weekly planner checked each Monda