g7-rds-check-facts | Unit 3, Snap judgments. The student checks two invented claims about what most seventh graders think against a small random sample, decides which claim the data back, and writes why each person might have believed theirs. Grade 7 adds why the other person believes what they do.
g7-sa-name-feelings | Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student names an invented character's inside feeling and the feeling the character shows, as another character would see it, rates both on the scale, and chooses words to ask about the gap. Grade 7 asks what one person's feeling looks like to another. Teacher note: checks name a character's feeling and its clue; no clinical words; a student's own feeling is never recorded.
g7-sa-feel-think-link | Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student fills two think-feel-do chains for the same invented moment, one for each person in it, and writes how each person's thought changed their feeling and action. This year the chain runs for both people. Teacher note: a thinking skill used on characters, not a treatment; no student is asked to test their own thoughts aloud.
g7-sm-big-feelings | Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. In an invented scene where a teammate's mistake loses a game, the student uses the calm-down steps, chooses a response, and writes how the teammate would hear each option. What grade 7 adds: how a response lands on the other person.
g7-sm-calm-down | Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student coaches a partner through choosing a tool from the menu for an invented stressful moment, notices the partner may need a different tool than their own, and writes how two friends could agree on a calm signal. New in grade 7: helping another person choose. Teacher note: breathing, stretching and pausing as plain body skills, with no spiritual framing. Stress, sleep and mental health topics are health's (health row g6-meh-stress-coping), named in the teacher notes. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
g7-sa-tell-truth | Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student chooses the true action in an invented case where a friend asks them to cover for a broken rule, writes how the friend and the teacher each see it, and names what honesty costs. Grade 7 sets honesty against loyalty to another person. Checked on the invented case only, never on a student's own conduct.
g7-sa-i-can-try | Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. When an invented friend doubts they can pass a hard test, the student names two steps the friend could take and one place to ask for help on the goal steps card, and writes what they would say to encourage. Grade 7 turns the skill outward, to another person's doubt. Teacher note: the course never records a student's own answer; checks use the character's plan.
g7-sa-skills-grow | Unit 4, Big feelings, steady choices. The student reads feedback a coach gave an invented player, takes the coach's view of what to change, and chooses the practice plan with the reason. Never belief alone. This year the fix starts from someone else's feedback.
g7-rel-talk-listen | Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student listens to a partner's account of an invented misunderstanding and sums it up until the partner agrees, then gives their own side with the talk turn card. Grade 7 makes the other person the judge of whether they were heard.
g7-rel-settle-disagreements | Unit 5, Repair and conflict. With a partner the student works an invented conflict by naming each side's interests, choosing a repair (an apology, a fix, a new plan), and saying the I-statement. New this year: the other side's interests shape the repair. The teacher plays any upset part.
g7-rel-ask-offer-help | Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student notices an invented friend who is struggling but has not asked, sizes the problem on the meter, chooses how to offer help, rehearses the offer with a partner playing the friend (who first turns it down), knows when to bring in an adult even if the friend says no, and writes why the chosen offer fits. Grade 7 is about offering from the other person's side. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor. Self-harm and crisis topics are health's (health row g7-meh-self-harm-help).
g7-soc-care-when-struggle | Unit 5, Repair and conflict. Before acting, the student asks what an invented struggling friend wants (company, help, space), and writes how the friend would feel about each kind of care. The friend's own wish now decides the care. Teacher note: if a student tells of harm or risk, follow health's steps (docs/courses/health/APPROACH.md, "If a student tells you something"): the school's reporting route and the counselor.
g7-soc-read-feelings | Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student reads the feelings of both people in an invented argument from their clues, checks in with each, and chooses words that respect each side. Grade 7 reads two people at once.
g7-soc-thankful | Unit 5, Repair and conflict. After an invented conflict is settled, the student thanks the other person for something they did in the repair, choosing words that person will take as sincere. New in grade 7: thanks as part of repair, aimed at how the other person hears it.
g7-rel-stand-up | Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student plans how to support the person treated unfairly in an invented case, asks what that person wants before acting, and chooses the upstander option they would welcome. The student rehearses the check-in question and the chosen words with the teacher, who plays the person treated unfairly and then the unkind act, while a partner watches for the steps; then the student writes why that option fits. Grade 7 adds the target's own view. Teacher note: no student plays the unkind part or the one treated unfairly; school life only, and no cause is chosen by the course.
g7-rel-lead-follow | Unit 5, Repair and conflict. The student leads a group through an invented disagreement as a neutral guide with the talk turn card, making sure each side is heard, then follows the group's choice. This year the leader holds everyone's view, not their own.
g7-sm-set-goal | Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student sets a shared goal with a partner on the goal steps card, weighs both partners' time against it, and changes the plan after a check-in where the partner's needs differ. Grade 7: a partner's needs reshape the choice.
g7-sm-plan-and-tidy | Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student plans an invented team event timeline where others' tasks depend on theirs, builds in a buffer, and writes how a late teammate changes the plan. What grade 7 adds: depending on other people.
g7-sm-step-forward | Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student starts a class project and invites a classmate who would not join on their own, writing how the project looks from that classmate's side. New this year: bringing another person in.
g7-sm-have-a-say | Unit 6, Taking the lead. The class drafts a proposal for a school change, and the student asks two groups it affects (younger students, staff) what they think and changes the proposal with the reason. Grade 7: the proposal answers other people's views.
g7-rds-solve-problems | Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student solves a school problem with an invented group that disagrees on its cause, records each person's view of the cause, and chooses a solution to test, writing the reason. Other people's views of the cause enter this year.
g7-rds-careful-thinking | Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student runs the five-step stop-think-choose with a partner who prefers a different option, and writes how hearing the partner's reasons changed or held the choice. Careful thinking now takes in another person's reasons.
g7-rds-good-for-us | Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student weighs an invented choice between a weekend team practice and a family chore day, for their own well-being and for the people on each side, writing how each would see it. Grade 7 gives the other people's well-being its own column.
g7-rds-who-is-affected | Unit 6, Taking the lead. The student maps who a class decision affects, then asks a partner playing one affected group for an effect they missed, and chooses with the reason. Grade 7 adds a voice from the affected side.

