 class are ready. The point-of-view model is introduced here, before grade 7 sets two narrators side by side. First-use retrieval: simile and metaphor (e5-figurative), how chapters fit (e5-whole-structure), how a narrator's view shapes events (e5-narrator-influence), visual and media elements (e5-visual-multimedia), two stories of one genre compared (e5-genre-compare) | Candidate: the play's text record (no computed measure; qualitative and task); a speaking look-for for the performance (RUBRIC-ELA 2j) | Fear in a storm, an old neighbour who is unwell; no death. The class performs the play; no student plays a person being harmed. Recordings are made for the course |
english-language-arts-grade-7 | | 1 | The salmon year. "When the river a family has fished for generations changes, what do they hold on to, and what do they let go?" | 30 | e7-cite-several; e7-theme-development; e7-story-elements-interact; e7-contrast-pov; e7-narrative-writing; e7-reading-range; e7-text-response-writing; e7-collab-discussion; e7-revise-purpose-audience; e7-commas-spelling | RL.7.1; RI.7.1; RL.7.2; RL.7.3; RL.7.6; W.7.3; RL.7.10; RI.7.10; W.7.9; W.7.10; SL.7.1; W.7.5; L.7.3; L.7.2 | Texts: an original Curio Class novella read whole (about 30,000 to 35,000 words, the extra pages read in the home reading; lengthened 2026-11-14 so the band's whole texts climb) on a present-day family that fishes the Klamath River, in the last year before its dams came down, told in alternating chapters by a brother and sister, 13 and 15 (rights: our own). The family is not written as Karuk or Yurok: the Tribes' voices come in their own published words in unit 6. Paired: the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration's fisheries pages on Klamath salmon and the US Geological Survey's river-flow pages for the Klamath (public domain, federal works). Independent reading: a long book of the student's choice from the Reading Room shelf or home, logged | Several pieces of evidence gathered across chapters for a point stated and one inferred (RL.7.1), and from the fisheries and river-flow pages (RI.7.1). A theme followed from where it is hinted to where it lands, with an objective summary (RL.7.2); how the river, the fish and the family's choices act on one another (RL.7.3); how the author develops and contrasts the two siblings' views (RL.7.6). The discussion routine: prepared with marked text (SL.7.1a), rules for collegial discussion followed, progress tracked toward specific goals and deadlines, and individual roles defined as needed (SL.7.1b), questions posed that elicit elaboration, and responses to others' questions and comments with relevant observations and ideas that bring the discussion back on topic as needed (SL.7.1c), views changed by evidence and said so (SL.7.1d). The evidence paragraph daily (W.7.9a; W.7.9b on the fisheries pages' claims), short and long (W.7.10). The band and the reading log (RL.7.10, RI.7.10). A narrative told by one of two narrators through the full process: orient with point of view (W.7.3a), dialogue, pacing and description (W.7.3b), transitions for time and setting (W.7.3c), precise and sensory words (W.7.3d), an ending that reflects (W.7.3e); revised for purpose and audience with peers and the teacher (W.7.5) and edited for wordiness (L.7.3a); commas between coordinate adjectives (L.7.2a) and spelling (L.7.2b) in their own drafts | A long shared book first, so several-evidence citing, theme development and the discussion routine are taught on one text everyone has read. It starts the year's second strand, the river and its dam, as story, which unit 6 returns to, so unit 6 reads the dam argument with this knowledge; unit 2's Dust Bowl is the year's one drought, and grade 5 holds the band's one ranch-family drought story. Two narrators carry the point-of-view model on from grade 6 unit 5. Retrieval tasks in the first two weeks: one well-chosen line per point and an objective summary (e6-cite-evidence, e6-theme-summary, e6-plot-episodes), one narrator's view on the model (e6-narrator-pov), the band and the log (e6-reading-range), the evidence paragraph and discussion roles (e6-text-response-writing, e6-collab-discussion), a narrative sample revised (e6-narrative-writing, e6-revise-edit), setting off an extra element (e6-punctuation-spelling) | Candidate: the novella's text record (RUBRIC-ELA 2b); the discussion look-for sheet (2i); an end task joining reading and writing on the theme (2v) | Money worries, a closed salmon season and a grandparent's illness; no death on the page. Students from fishing and farm families may be in the room and are not asked to share their own. The novella gets the text agent's read and the sensitivity read, after the Karuk and Yurok study the brief names, since the river is theirs |
english-language-arts-grade-7 | | 3 | Smallpox: who gets the credit? "Who should get the credit for beating smallpox, and how do authors decide?" | 26 | e7-authors-shape; e7-research-questions; e7-present-salient; e7-word-choice-info; e7-word-strategies; e7-produce-publish | RI.7.9; W.7.7; W.7.8; SL.7.4; SL.7.5; SL.7.6; RI.7.4; L.7.6; L.7.4; L.7.5b; W.7.4; W.7.6 | Texts: two original articles on how smallpox was beaten, one crediting Edward Jenner and one crediting Onesimus, Lady Mary Wortley Montagu and the people who inoculated first; Centers for Disease Control and Prevention pages on smallpox and its eradication in 1980 (public domain, federal works). Paired primary excerpts: Cotton Mather on Onesimus (his July 12, 1716 letter to Woodward), Montagu's letter from Constantinople (1717, published 1763), Jenner's Inquiry (1798), all public domain. A fenced research set of about twelve federal pages on cholera, yellow fever, polio and measles, and one weak source written for the course (an advertisement for a "miracle tonic", no real brand) | How two authors writing on the same topic shape it by emphasising different evidence and advancing different readings (RI.7.9), taught on the two original articles; the paired 1717, 1721 and 1798 excerpts are then read for which evidence each article chose. Word meaning and the impact of word choice on tone in the articles (RI.7.4); the unit's academic and medical words (L.7.6). Words worked out by context (L.7.4a), new Greek and Latin parts (immun, epi, pan, demos, logy), spread from unit 3 through units 4 and 6 with weekly retrieval (L.7.4b), general and specialized reference materials, print and digital, used to find a word's pronunciation, precise meaning or part of speech: a general dictionary, a medical glossary and a thesaurus compared on words from the unit (L.7.4c), and checking (L.7.4d); synonym, antonym and analogy to sharpen a meaning (L.7.5b). A short research project on how one disease was fought: a question from a list, several sources, further focused questions generated (W.7.7), in groups that set goals and deadlines for their research and check progress at the end of each week (SL.7.1b, used again); print and digital sources searched with good terms, credibility and accuracy judged (the tonic advertisement is the one to catch), quoted and paraphrased without plagiarism, cited in a standard format (W.7.8). Written as an article planned for task, purpose and audience (W.7.4), with transitions that create cohesion (W.7.2c, used again); drafted in a shared document where partners comment and the writer responds and revises, and published with live links to its sources (W.7.6: produce, publish, link to and cite sources, interact and collaborate; on a shared classroom device or a lab session, no student account). A talk on the findings with salient points in a focused, coherent way, pertinent facts and examples, eye contact and clear speech (SL.7.4), multimedia and a visual display that clarify claims and findings and emphasize salient points (SL.7.5), rehearsed as an informal explanation to a partner and then given formally, language and delivery adapted between the two (SL.7.6) | Research comes before social studies grade 7's Greek research (its unit 9, from day 101), so ELA models the general routine first. Comparing authors on one topic needs the central-ideas work of unit 2. The roots of medical words begin the Latin and Greek roots social studies uses in its Latin legacy unit (about day 134). Retrieval tasks: two accounts of one event (e6-compare-accounts), a grade 6 research question refocused and a basic source list (e6-short-research), a short talk in logical order (e6-present-claims), a typed page with peer comments (e6-produce-publish), technical words and roots (e6-technical-words, e6-word-strategies) | Candidate: research taught and then practiced on more than one source (RUBRIC-ELA 2s, 2t); the word list met again in a later unit (2k, 2l) | Smallpox killed many; stated plainly, no images of the sick. Onesimus was enslaved; Mather owned him; stated plainly. The unit credits Onesimus by name and does not dwell on his enslavement beyond what the credit question needs; the sensitivity agent reads the unit after the study the brief names. Native nations' losses to smallpox are stated as fact, not as a lesson in blame. No student is asked about vaccines in their own family; the CDC pages are the facts used. The tonic advertisement is labeled as written for the course |
