rify the relationships among claims, reasons and evidence (W.7.1c), formal style (W.7.1d), a conclusion (W.7.1e); typed, about two pages. The story is also read against the feature: what it kept and invented (RL.7.9, used again) | Beside social studies grade 7 unit 8 (days 87 to 100 plus held day 120), which asks whether Athens was a democracy; ELA reads how one speech made the case, and its opening feature teaches the assembly and citizenship before the story or the excerpts need them. Argument comes before social studies unit 12 (about day 145), which asks for it. Author's position after unit 3, where authors' shaping was taught; the year's seminar on Pericles uses SL.7.1c's elaboration questions again. Retrieval tasks: author's purpose and a traced argument (e6-author-purpose, e6-trace-argument), the grade 6 argument plan (e6-argument-writing), reading against hearing and watching (e6-read-vs-watch) | Candidate: the argument rubric used all year (RUBRIC-ELA 2p); a speaking look-for (2j) | Athens excluded women, enslaved people and foreigners from the assembly; stated plainly. The youth-council question is about a town's choice, not a school election, and no student is asked to campaign. The 1912 speech is a campaign speech; students are told so |
