english-language-arts-grade-8 | | 2 | Founding words, answered. "When the founders wrote that all men are created equal, who answered them, and what did they say?" | 32 | e8-word-choice-info; e8-delineate-argument; e8-conflicting-viewpoints; e8-conflicting-texts; e8-argument-writing; e8-pause-omission | RI.8.4; L.8.6; RI.8.8; SL.8.3; RI.8.6; RI.8.9; W.8.1; L.8.2 | Texts: anchors (rights: our own unless named): two original features, one on Benjamin Banneker and his almanac, one on the 1793 yellow fever in Philadelphia and the Free African Society's volunteers; two original editorials on what the town's marker for the 1793 volunteers should say, written to answer each other, one with irrelevant evidence; two short original news reports of the same town meeting that disagree on a fact and on its meaning; an original recorded talk at that meeting. Paired background: National Archives pages on the Declaration and Constitution (public domain, federal works). Paired primary excerpts, short and read with full support (all public domain): the Declaration's second paragraph and grievances; the Constitution's Preamble; Banneker's letter to Jefferson (1791) and Jefferson's reply; Mathew Carey, A Short Account of the Malignant Fever (1793); Absalom Jones and Richard Allen, A Narrative of the Proceedings of the Black People (1794) | Word meaning and the effect of word choice, including analogies and allusions, on tone (RI.8.4): taught on the features and editorials, then applied to the Declaration, read for its word choice ("self-evident") and for who answered it, with Banneker's letter, and its allusion to the Declaration, as the answer; the unit's academic words (L.8.6). An argument delineated and judged, with irrelevant evidence named (RI.8.8): taught on the two editorials, then applied to Banneker's letter, and the same on the recorded talk (SL.8.3). How an author acknowledges and responds to conflicting evidence or viewpoints (RI.8.6): the two editorials first, then Banneker and Jefferson each answering the other. Two texts that disagree on facts and on interpretation (RI.8.9): the two news reports first, then Carey set beside Jones and Allen. A short argument in one sitting first, on Carey's account. Then an argument through the full process, "Our town is putting up a marker for the 1793 volunteers: what should it say?": claim, with counterclaims named and answered (W.8.1a), relevant evidence from the sources (W.8.1b), words that link claims, counterclaims and reasons (W.8.1c), formal style (W.8.1d), a conclusion (W.8.1e); typed, about three pages. Commas, ellipsis and the dash for a pause or break (L.8.2a; a dash appears only in exercise sentences and the texts), the ellipsis to mark words left out of a quotation (L.8.2b), spelling (L.8.2c) | The month after civics 8 unit 2 (the Declaration, days 16 to 30), so the document is not first met here and never in the same month; civics reads it for its ideas, ELA for word choice and response. The Declaration is read again in ELA grade 10 (the liberty set) and grade 11 (its reasoning) and grade 9 reads seminal documents for rhetoric; grade 8 reads it in an excerpt. Argument with counterclaims comes before civics unit 6's court research (about day 77). Point of view against other views comes well before civics unit 9 (about day 131). 32 days: the year's hardest paired texts and its process argument. Retrieval tasks: word choice and tone in an article (e7-word-choice-info), an author's position and a judged argument (e7-author-position, e7-evaluate-argument), two authors on one topic (e7-authors-shape), the grade 7 argument plan (e7-argument-writing), commas between coordinate adjectives (e7-commas-spelling) | Candidate: the 80 percent count of text-dependent questions (RUBRIC-ELA 2g); the argument rubric used all year, with a model piece (2p) | Slavery and the Declaration's exclusions stated plainly; Jefferson enslaved people while answering Banneker. Carey's charge against the Black volunt