FRAME computer-science-grade-6
UNIT 1 Programs with memory. *How does a program remember, change and reuse what it knows, and how do we build one as a team?* Anchor: a short rain-gauge program shown in blocks and in the text view, which keeps a running total of a week of readings and says when a day was the wettest so far | 16 | g6-variable-types; g6-procedures; g6-inclusive-collab | MS-PRO-VD-16; MS-PRO-PD-12; MS-PRO-PD-15 | Concept area: programming. Practices: CT7 (a procedure as a reusable part); CT8 (plan the program before building it); CT9 (test with a test-table, change one thing, retest); IC4 (a team agreement, shared goals and checkpoints); IC5 (driver and navigator roles that switch on the timer); IC3 (written feedback to another team) | Session 1 (unplugged): be the computer on a paper program in both views; a variable is a named box that holds one value of one type at a time: a number, a string of text, a true or false value (Boolean); assignment read "becomes". Session 2 (device): our editor shows blocks and the text view side by side; a worked program is traced, then run; values stored and read back. Session 3 (device, modify): change and combine values (add to a number, join two strings, flip a Boolean); a typed "5" and the number 5 are not the same, shown by a quick error, and one value converted to another type. Session 4 (trace): a choice that reads a variable (if the total is over 20, if raining is true). Session 5 (trace): a count-controlled repeat that updates a variable each time round, traced row by row. Session 6 (device, modify): the anchor modified to count rainy days, track the largest and smallest reading and count down a timer (increment, decrement, running total, maximum and minimum, counting). Session 7 (device, make): pairs build a short program to a brief (a plant-watering log or a game score with a bonus rule) with three look-fors and a three-input test-table as review. Session 8 (trace, paper): a program with the same four lines written three times beside the version with one named procedure and three calls; a bad name and a good name. Session 9 (device, modify): fill the blanks in a procedure version, then replace repeated code in a second program with calls. Session 10 (device, make): find the repeated or grouped steps in a given program, make a procedure, call it, and write two lines on how it helps (less repeated code, one place to update). Session 11 (unplugged): the team agreement (every idea heard, everyone types, roles switch), feedback frames, and the team's plan on paper with each member's idea recorded. Sessions 12 to 14 (device, make): the team program to a brief (a class garden helper with variables of two types, a choice, a repeat and a procedure), drafted, given written feedback by another team with the checklist, revised and retested; the team writes who spoke, who typed and what they changed. Out: no lists or other collections (grade 7's g7-lists), no procedures with parameters or return values (high school's g9-optimize-procedures), no professional syntax beyond what the editor shows, no formal team methods. End-of-unit (day 15): unseen items: trace a program with a number, a string and a Boolean; predict a choice on a variable; finish a running-total trace; spot a type error; rewrite a repeated block as a procedure on paper; a short build or debug task in the editor (paper version); the team program is scored from its revised version (session 14) against the rubric, with the reflection; respond day 16. Tools: our own editor (blocks and text view side by side, step-through trace, stated tests with "yes" or "not yet" per look-for; job: build, trace and test every program; tool ask for TOOL-ASKS.md). Curio Class writes: the anchor and every worked, blank-filled and buggy program in both views with tested solutions, trace-table sheets, the team brief, rubric and feedback checklist
NOTES Pairs are assigned, not chosen, and roles switch on the timer so no student holds the keyboard all period; feedback names the program, never the person. The brief's data are about things (rain, plants, a game), never a student. Devices: cords behind the table. Agent reads: sensitivity.
