FRAME computer-science-grade-7
UNIT 6 Computing changes things. *When a new computing technology arrives, who decides whether to use it, and what changes that nobody planned?* Anchor: the automatic telephone exchange that replaced operators who connected calls by hand (facts from the Computer History Museum timeline, computerhistory.org/timeline/, and the teacher page's dated sources) | 7 | g7-impact-history; g7-when-to-use-ai; g7-automation-work | MS-SOC-HI-39; MS-SOC-ET-40; MS-SOC-CE-45 | Concept area: computing and society. Practices: ESR1 (weigh who gains and who loses); IC5 (a team's shared timeline); CT6 (state what a task needs before choosing a tool); HCD10 (who a technology serves) | Session 56 (unplugged): one historical computing technology (the automatic exchange, the personal computer, or a large government database) traced on a timeline with two arrows, its intended effects and its unintended ones on people (communication, jobs, privacy); retrieval of grade 6's eras timeline. Session 57: its effects on the environment (energy used, e-waste), from a dated source (the Environmental Protection Agency's electronics donation and recycling page). Session 58 (unplugged): automation case cards (checkout lines, bank tellers, farm machines): which tasks were replaced, which jobs were created, what workers had to learn, with dated figures from the Bureau of Labor Statistics; retrieval of grade 6's careers. Session 59 (society): two views on one automation case, safety and accuracy gained against jobs lost, and how workers and industries adapt (retraining, new roles). Session 60 (unplugged): what AI, robots and virtual reality can and cannot do now, and what each needs (power, a network, reliability, readiness); retrieval of grade 6's human decisions. Session 61: a task set (grade essays, recognize handwriting, sort mail, decide a school punishment) judged on a chart with two columns, a verdict and a "person decides" flag, by capability, limits and environmental cost; the human choice and its consequences is MS-SOC-HU-43 used again, owned in grade 6. Session 62 (society): a written claim on whether one emerging technology suits a named school problem, with a partner's written feedback. Out: cost-benefit sums and environmental calculations, forecasting jobs or future breakthroughs, how the technologies work inside, debates on regulation and rights (grade 8's g8-emerging-debate). End-of-unit: the year-end course-level check (day 63) stands for it. Tools: none (all unplugged). Curio Class writes: the exchange timeline and its sources, the environment page, the automation case cards with dated figures, the task set and verdict chart
NOTES Automation can touch a family's work: no student is asked about a family member's job; a student's own offer is welcome. Telephone operators were mostly women: say so from the source, without blame. Two views and a dated source for each case; no company named. Agent reads: sensitivity (the starting pack in Sensitivity reads first).
